1. Linux Command Execution and Hardware Investigation
Aim

To understand how Linux executes a command entered by a user by creating a child process and using system calls for process creation, program execution, and synchronization.

Concepts Used
fork() – Creates a child process.
exec() – Replaces the child process with the requested program.
wait() – Allows the parent process to wait for the child.
getpid() – Displays the current process ID.
getppid() – Displays the parent process ID.
Working

The user enters a Linux command.

The program then:

Creates a child process using fork().
The child executes the command using an exec() system call.
The parent continues separately.
The parent waits for the child using wait().
The PID and PPID of the processes are displayed.
Process Flow
User enters command
        ↓
      fork()
      ↙   ↘
 Parent   Child
   ↓        ↓
 wait()   exec()
   ↓        ↓
   ↓    Linux command
   ↘      ↙
   Child completes
        ↓
      Parent
Hardware and OS Investigation

The following Linux commands are used:

uname

Provides information about the Linux kernel, operating system, architecture, and kernel version.

lscpu

Displays CPU-related information such as architecture, cores, threads, and CPU model.

lsblk

Displays storage devices, disks, and partitions available to the system.

ps

Displays information about currently running processes.

top

Provides real-time information about CPU usage, memory usage, processes, and system load.

Relationship Between Hardware and OS
Hardware Resource	OS Abstraction
CPU	Processes and threads
RAM	Virtual memory
Storage	Files and file systems
Keyboard	Input devices
Monitor	Output devices
Network card	Network interface
Other hardware	Device drivers

The operating system acts as an interface between applications and physical hardware.

Application
     ↓
System Call
     ↓
Operating System Kernel
     ↓
Device Driver
     ↓
Hardware

